WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.600
[Narrator:] The first certified P4 containment facility in the United States for recombinant DNA research,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.180
a laboratory designed to meet the requirements set forth 

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:47.100
in the NIH guidelines governing recombinant DNA research, is now available to scientists.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.640
The laboratory itself is under negative air pressure.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.280
The experiments are carried out from beginning to end

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:58.200
 inside an elaborate system of enclosed gas-tight cabinets.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:02.800
This means that nothing, not even the tiniest particle of dust or air,

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.750
or the smallest micro-organism,

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:09.120
can penetrate the heavy stainless steel and glass cabinets.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.650
This system of cabinets measures about seventy feet in combined length.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:19.720
It is contained inside a room which has an area of about four hundred square feet.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:25.150
Using the shoulder-length rubber gloves, which are tightly attached to the sides of the cabinet,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:32.130
laboratory workers are able to move and control all the materials and tools used in the experiment 

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:39.820
without direct contact between their hands and anything inside the cabinet.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.740
Each experiment must be carefully planned well in advance,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.460
since all of the materials used in the experiment,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:50.630
including chemicals, cultures, and laboratory equipment, must be brought 

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:56.060
into the group of interconnected cabinets, or cabinet line, as it is called.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:03.480
One of the methods of bringing materials into the cabinet is through an autoclave, or steam sterilizer.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:08.380
Equipment and materials are loaded onto a shelf inside the autoclave

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.220
for transfer to the interior of the cabinet lines.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:20.420
A heavy door is then swung into place and locked to provide a tight seal.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:25.230
A laboratory worker removes them from the cabinet side of the autoclave. 

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.590
Because the interior of the device has now been exposed

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:30.530
to the inside of the cabinet,

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.780
it will be necessary to run the autoclave

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:41.840
through a sterilizing cycle before it is opened again on its outside end.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.340
The two-hundred and fifty degree Fahrenheit steam pressure cycle takes time. 

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:55.690
All large items have to enter through the autoclave, so planning its maximum use is essential.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.160
The cabinet lines are modular, that is,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:04.700
made up of individual connected units allowing the free movement of materials within the lines.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:10.340
At least two workers are required for most manipulations and movements of materials.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:15.130
A variety of equipment for experiments must be contained within the cabinets,

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:23.450
and that includes a refrigerator which is used for both long- and short-term storage of materials.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:34.670
Here, supplies of growth media are being checked and readied for later use in preparing bacterial culture.

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:41.120
Another design feature of the refrigerator is movable shelves which slide in and out

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.580
to allow the laboratory worker easier access to materials stored within.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.580
Note the tools used to extend the limited reach of the researcher

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:58.160
as he works within the restricted space of the gas-tight cabinets.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:06.000
The cabinet system in this laboratory has two levels which are connected by a small elevator or dumbwaiter.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.540
Specially weakened strains of bacteria,

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.920
which cannot survive outside the carefully controlled laboratory conditions,

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:18.480
are grown in a waterbath located on the second tier of the system. 

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.500
Once the cultures have been grown and are ready for use in the experiment,

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.230
they are transferred to the nearby elevator,

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:29.390
which descends with them to the lower level.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:37.260
Here, most of the biochemical and microbiological steps in the experiment are actually carried out.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:42.010
The weakened strains of bacteria which are used in recombinant DNA research

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:46.470
make up a second form of containment called biological containment.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.020
Although physical barriers or physical containment

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:55.260
are highly effective in preventing the escape of micro-organisms,

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.630
biological containment adds a built-in safeguard.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:05.650
The crippled bacteria are so weak that they self-destruct outside the laboratory.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:13.650
These two types of barriers, physical and biological, are always used in combination.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:18.750
This particular cabinet system has an area with glove ports on both sides.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.990
This arrangement allows two laboratory workers to team up

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.710
for some of the more delicate operations,

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:31.060
such as the transfer of cultures or reagents from one container to another.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:38.420
Continuing the inventory of research tools mounted within the cabinet line is a high speed centrifuge.

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:42.110
This device, mounted flush with the bottom space of the cabinet,

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.710
is used in purifying DNA.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:50.930
Spinning rapidly, DNA separates from protein and solvent. 

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:57.830
The control panel for this centrifuge is mounted outside the barrier of the gas-tight cabinets.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.220
Bacteria for experiments such as this are grown in single colonies 

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:07.780
which are cultivated in standard laboratory petri dishes.

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:13.710
The petri dishes are kept in an incubator inside the cabinet line.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.200
The microscope is a vital tool in experiments involving micro-organisms.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:23.300
An instrument is mounted in this laboratory's line.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:27.520
Despite the restraints that are imposed by the heavy rubber gloves,

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.440
skilled laboratory workers are able to make delicate adjustments on the microscope.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:38.420
A closed-circuit television system with the screen mounted just above the researcher's head 

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.620
provides the means for viewing.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:50.520
A second means of entering the enclosed cabinet line is through a device called a dunk tank.

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:56.970
This consists of a tank filled with a strong liquid disinfectant and is an integral part of the barrier,

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:03.410
and like the autoclave, prevents the passage of unwanted living organisms in either direction.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:11.650
A leakproof container is used to convey materials needed inside the line, safely through the dunk tank.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:17.260
Note how the attached gloves are pulled back inside the cabinet.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:23.350
This is due to the negative air pressure that is constantly maintained within the cabinets.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:30.140
This added safety feature ensures that no organism escapes, should the gloves be punctured.

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:36.500
Although use of experimental animals is not a significant part of most recombinant DNA research,

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:42.580
this laboratory is equipped to house animals on two tiers within the cabinets.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:49.550
Experimental mice like these can be inoculated and examined within the cabinets.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:55.710
The daily care and feeding of the animals is also carried out entirely within the cabinet system.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:08:02.040
Materials needed to work with and care for the animals are introduced through the dunk tank.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:08.490
When necessary, cages and waste are removed in the same fashion that all other material is taken out: 

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.390
through an autoclave.

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:17.660
The material is placed inside and is then heated to a temperature sufficient to kill all organisms.

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.090
At the end of a sterilization cycle,

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:24.190
material is removed from the autoclave for further processing,

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:27.760
either washing if it is reusable laboratory equipment,

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:32.440
or disposal as trash if it is not reusable.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:37.520
As any material leaves the building, it is sterilized a second time.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:45.590
Material is loaded into the autoclave and again heated to achieve complete sterilization.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:53.020
We have seen the facilities, equipment, and techniques that will be used in this laboratory.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:55.570
Now let's take a close look at the substance

00:08:55.570 --> 00:09:01.980
which is the focus of the scientific activity which will be carried out here.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.380
In order to show more clearly some of the methods of DNA research,

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:10.830
we are going through a non-recombinant DNA procedure.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.210
Close-up pictures, which you will see,

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:19.070
of a bacterial DNA culture were made under P3 conditions.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:25.900
Reagents are added to the purified DNA to separate it from other materials

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:32.210
such as protein, and to prepare it for spinning.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:39.270
A glass rod can then be used to spin the purified DNA into a cohesive mass.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:44.360
Pressed against the side of the test tube to eliminate excess moisture,

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:49.160
the long strands of DNA take on the appearance of transparent thread,

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:52.500
wrapped around the end of the glass rod.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:59.340
The DNA molecules may be dissolved again in a salt solution

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:06.750
for further study and analysis of the DNA's genetic or biochemical make-up if so desired.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:19.100
At this stage the DNA is ready for spinning or other processing as may be called for in the experiment.

